{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unfit-beneficial-whimbrel","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\nThe mcp entrypoint added by `kilted-nuclear-bronco` clones the `tickets` branch SYNCHRONOUSLY\nbefore `exec \"$@\"`. That branch has **199,846 commits**. The blue-green readiness gate allows\n**120 seconds** (`autodeploy.sh:130` `MCP_HEALTH_TIMEOUT=120`). The clone cannot finish in time,\nso the server never starts listening, `/health` never answers, and autodeploy removes the new\ncontainer and rolls back — on every deploy.\n\n**`main` currently cannot deploy the mcp service at all.** This is a regression from my own\nmerged change, not a pre-existing fault.\n\n## Evidence (from the box, 2026-08-26)\n\n    AUTODEPLOY_ERROR {\"reason\": \"mcp-unhealthy\", \"detail\": \"new mcp container\n      rebar-mcp-11f6e61c732b-8093 failed /health within 120s; removing it, leaving the OLD\n      upstream live\"}\n    AUTODEPLOY_ERROR {\"reason\": \"deploy_failed\", \"detail\": \"target=11f6e61c732b... fail#1 backoff=60s\"}\n\n`11f6e61c732b` is exactly \"feat(mcp): provision the mcp ticket store\" (Gerrit 2231).\n\n    deployed-sha:    078ca376cee3...            (predates the merge)\n    running:         rebar-mcp-078ca376cee3-8092  Up 43 minutes (healthy)\n    nginx upstream:  server 127.0.0.1:8092;       (correct — pointing at the OLD container)\n    tickets branch:  199,846 commits\n\nThe nginx upstream is CORRECT and the old container is healthy, so the rollback works exactly as\ndesigned. The endpoint is serving stale code because the new code cannot boot.\n\n## Root cause of the design error\n\nI modelled the entrypoint on the review-bot's (`Dockerfile.reviewbot:143-166`), which also\nclones at entrypoint. But the review-bot has NO blue-green readiness deadline — it is a\nstop-and-start service. The mcp service is promoted only after answering `/health` within a hard\n120s window. Copying a blocking-clone pattern into a service with a readiness gate is what broke\nit. The soft-failure posture I designed (\"no PAT ⇒ clone deferred, container still boots\") only\ncovers the ABSENT-credential case; with the credential PRESENT the clone runs and blocks.\n\n## Fix\n\nDo not block startup on the clone. Start the server first and converge the store in the\nbackground, so the container becomes healthy immediately and the store appears when it is ready.\n\nThis composes correctly with the read guard already on main: while the clone is still running,\ntracker reads raise `store_uninitialized` — an honest, machine-readable \"not ready yet\" — rather\nthan returning `[]` and pretending the tracker is empty. That is exactly the degradation the\nread guard was built to provide.\n\nAlso worth bounding the clone cost itself (199k commits is expensive to re-clone on every fresh\ncontainer), but correctness first: unblock the boot.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The mcp container answers `/health` well within `MCP_HEALTH_TIMEOUT` regardless of whether\n      a tickets clone is in progress, so blue-green promotion succeeds.\n- [ ] The store still converges: once the clone completes, tracker reads return real tickets.\n- [ ] While the clone is in flight, tracker reads report `store_uninitialized` rather than `[]`.\n- [ ] A clone failure still leaves the container serving (soft posture preserved).\n- [ ] Regression coverage asserts the entrypoint does not block `exec` on the clone.\n\n## Verification\n\n    .venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/unit/test_mcp_on_box_infra.py -q -p no:randomly\n    bash -n on the generated entrypoint\n\nThe test has teeth when: restoring the synchronous clone makes the new assertion FAIL.","id":"b3aa-b77b-cf87-4481","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["infra","mcp","deploy","regression"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"mcp entrypoint blocks boot on a 199k-commit tickets clone, so every blue-green deploy fails the 120s health gate and rolls back"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787741571583986001,"uuid":"87a50c12-689f-471a-9b76-bd9a15c4d3f2"}